sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch17-03> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because dissimilar related fishes can be referred as wholes to the same figure in different coordinate systems, variation has proceeded on definite and orderly lines under a comprehensive law of growth affecting the whole structure, rather than by independent variation of separate parts as the usual conception of natural selection assumes.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Evolution varies the whole body in correlation, not character by character";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVII, “On the Theory of Transformations, or the Comparison of Related Forms”; topic “Theory of transformations”. Scope: evolutionary differentiation of related animal species"
}
